## Realism / weak recovery
Good: `net.JoinHostPort("[2001:db8::17]", "443")` mapping to `[[2001:db8::17]]:443` is a real net footgun (JoinHostPort wraps IPv6 itself); always wrapping `[host]:port` is the equally tempting board-shaped wrong fix and the mixed-host test names the contract (`[2001:db8::17]:443` plus bare `10.4.2.17:443`, not `[10.4.2.17]:443`). Copying `Authorization` onto every urllib redirect is the usual CDN-leak trap; stripping the header on every hop still cannot satisfy a test that requires same-host 307 to keep `Bearer harbor-ge`. Stale 502 fallback now compares dest want one IPv6 pair / CDN null against a second file still on the double wrap / leaked token (r61 densification). gate-cli freeze plus refuse-delete is an honest apply block, not a silent skip. Weak: the Go measure step still shells a python print as a stand-in before go test; hop_authorization is a designed probe rather than a recorded HTTPRedirectHandler dump. Next densification: a reviewer asking to keep always-wrap "so runbook dests always look like bracketed socket addrs", or a 502 whose local redirect-traces fixture is rewritten after the netloc patch and still shows the CDN Bearer.

